# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Local development
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://localhost:5000
NEXT_PUBLIC_SOCKET_URL=http://localhost:5000

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Hosted deployment (Vercel and friends)
#
# This file is an example only — client/.env is gitignored and never reaches the
# host, so these must be set in the project's environment-variable settings.
#
# Pick ONE of the two API wiring options:
#
#   A. Proxy (recommended). Leave NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL unset and set:
#          API_ORIGIN=https://your-api-host.example.com
#      The browser calls the site's own origin and next.config.mjs forwards
#      /api/* to the backend. No CORS setup, no mixed content, and the value is
#      read at runtime so it survives a config change without a code change.
#
#   B. Direct. Set:
#          NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=https://your-api-host.example.com
#      The browser calls the API host directly. It must be HTTPS (an HTTPS page
#      cannot call HTTP), and the API's CLIENT_URL / CLIENT_URLS must list this
#      site's origin or CORS will reject every request.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_* is inlined at BUILD time: changing it in the dashboard does
# nothing until you redeploy.
#
# Realtime is optional. Socket.IO needs a direct long-lived connection, so it
# cannot use the proxy and cannot run on a serverless host. Leave
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SOCKET_URL unset unless the API runs somewhere that supports
# WebSockets — every live view falls back to polling on its own.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# API_ORIGIN=https://your-api-host.example.com
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SOCKET_URL=https://your-api-host.example.com
